WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.109
Circuit breakers.

2
00:00:01.389 --> 00:00:02.904
Retries handle a blip.

3
00:00:03.184 --> 00:00:17.029
When a dependency is down for minutes, retrying every request makes things worse: each request waits through its full timeout and backoff before failing, threads and connections pile up, and the extra traffic slows recovery.

4
00:00:17.309 --> 00:00:22.216
A circuit breaker, as Martin Fowler describes it, wraps the call and tracks failures.

5
00:00:22.496 --> 00:00:28.363
While closed, calls pass through and consecutive failures are counted; a success resets the count.

6
00:00:28.643 --> 00:00:38.925
When failures reach a threshold, the breaker opens: calls fail immediately without touching the dependency, which is exactly what you want when you have a fallback model waiting.

7
00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:43.536
After a reset timeout, it goes half-open and lets one trial call through.

8
00:00:43.816 --> 00:00:47.656
Success closes it; failure reopens it and restarts the timer.

9
00:00:47.936 --> 00:00:56.725
Fowler also recommends logging every state change and alerting when a breaker trips, because a breaker that opens often is telling you something.

10
00:00:57.005 --> 00:00:58.371
Breakers have critics.

11
00:00:58.651 --> 00:01:08.955
The AWS article notes that they add modal behavior that is harder to test and can slow recovery, and suggests local retry budgets as an alternative.

12
00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:22.803
The two are compatible: use a retry budget to bound retry load, and a breaker per model to skip a dependency that is clearly down, so requests go straight to the fallback instead of spending their deadline on a dead primary.

13
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:30.293
Whatever you choose, test the state transitions, which is easy only if the breaker reads time from a clock you can control.
